WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:04.379
In this video, we'll look at porting our content

2
00:00:04.379 --> 00:00:08.320
out of our Unity project and into our new Unreal Engine project.

3
00:00:08.919 --> 00:00:12.580
So this diagram is pulled from the Epic Games

4
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:15.080
Unity to Unreal documentation page.

5
00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:20.260
We see a comparison of asset types inside of Unreal

6
00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:23.679
and asset types inside of Unity and their supported formats.

7
00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:27.179
The key things to note here are going to be some of the ones

8
00:00:27.179 --> 00:00:28.760
that we covered already.

9
00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:31.660
A Unity Mesh is a Static Mesh in Unreal Engine,

10
00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:35.359
a Skinned Mesh is a Skeletal Mesh in Unreal Engine.

11
00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:39.320
Animations are referred to as animation sequences,

12
00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:43.000
but textures, audio files, video files, fonts,

13
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.219
GLTF and SpeedTree models are all called the same thing.

14
00:00:48.399 --> 00:00:51.659
So now let's take a look at the Unity FBX Exporter.

15
00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:56.020
If you have FBX models from your artist team

16
00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:57.219
that you're working with already,

17
00:00:57.219 --> 00:00:58.920
you'll be able to import those directly.

18
00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:02.960
But if you have content that's been created inside of Unity,

19
00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:07.659
or you want to group a cluster of FBXs together into a new one,

20
00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:11.560
then we can do that with the Unity FBX Exporter.

21
00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.959
Similarly, if you've authored any animations inside of Unity,

22
00:01:14.959 --> 00:01:16.859
and you want those animations to come through

23
00:01:16.859 --> 00:01:21.260
with their relevant objects or, yeah, their relevant Meshes,

24
00:01:21.879 --> 00:01:23.760
the Unity FBX Exporter

25
00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.260
is how we can keep everything grouped together.

26
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:32.760
It's important to note, though, that lights, cameras, volumes,

27
00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:35.299
and custom script components

28
00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:38.099
are not going to be able to come through.

29
00:01:38.959 --> 00:01:40.799
The only things that we can get across

30
00:01:40.799 --> 00:01:46.060
are going to be Mesh Assets, animations, and media files.

31
00:01:47.299 --> 00:01:49.620
So let's take a look.

32
00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:54.709
Here in Unity, we have our collection of Meshes

33
00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:58.109
called State that I've named Stage Objects.

34
00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:02.189
This is a collection of seven cubes

35
00:02:02.189 --> 00:02:04.950
and a platform that our dancer is standing on.

36
00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:08.229
So we can bring this over as one asset.

37
00:02:08.789 --> 00:02:10.789
We have them selected here.

38
00:02:10.789 --> 00:02:16.000
We can right-click, go to Export FBX.

39
00:02:16.759 --> 00:02:23.000
We can call these, I'll call these Session Stage Objects.

40
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.259
I'll pick the folder I want them exported into.

41
00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:28.000
This is this exported folder.

42
00:02:29.469 --> 00:02:32.069
We have our source transform object.

43
00:02:32.069 --> 00:02:34.370
In this case, I only want the models.

44
00:02:36.479 --> 00:02:38.259
ASCII is good for that.

45
00:02:39.719 --> 00:02:43.639
I had one named Key Instances. Perfect.

46
00:02:43.639 --> 00:02:45.530
So I'll hit Export.

47
00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:47.930
And now if we look in our Exports folder,

48
00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:50.629
we have our Session Stage Objects.

49
00:02:51.189 --> 00:02:54.449
You also see another one from where we were doing this earlier.

50
00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:58.650
So for the next one, we are going to export our demo dancer.

51
00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:06.020
So even we can right-click on her, Export to FBX.

52
00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:10.479
We can go down to Models Only

53
00:03:10.479 --> 00:03:16.840
and switch this to Models Plus Animation and Skin Mesh.

54
00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:20.500
Everything else should be good to go.

55
00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:25.159
Hit Export. Instead of overriding, we'll call this Session.

56
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.139
And here she is. Fantastic.

57
00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:40.240
So now that we have, we can go ahead and save our unit.

58
00:03:40.879 --> 00:03:46.300
But for instance, in this case, we have a timeline in here.

59
00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:48.300
We have our stage lights.

60
00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:51.039
We have a camera, directional light.

61
00:03:51.039 --> 00:03:55.240
These things are not going to come through into Unreal.

62
00:03:55.240 --> 00:04:01.099
So we unfortunately will need to rebuild either the logic around those

63
00:04:01.099 --> 00:04:05.639
or the reposition them, add them to their equivalents

64
00:04:05.639 --> 00:04:11.780
to our level inside of Unreal session content.

65
00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:14.330
There it is.

66
00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:19.129
So as we saw when we were exporting our assets out of Unity,

67
00:04:19.129 --> 00:04:21.089
when you're exporting skinned meshes,

68
00:04:21.089 --> 00:04:25.290
which are going to be character assets with their animations,

69
00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:28.730
make sure you have Models Plus Animation selected

70
00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:31.790
and make sure your export path is also configured correctly.

71
00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:35.790
For regular meshes, just make sure in the options

72
00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:39.730
that it's set to Models Only, and you should be good to go.

73
00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:43.959
Another thing I'd like to call out here

74
00:04:43.959 --> 00:04:49.699
before we look at importing our FBXs into Unreal

75
00:04:49.699 --> 00:04:51.699
is the Unity Reporter.

76
00:04:51.699 --> 00:04:56.060
This is very similar to Unreal Engine's Tape Reporter feature.

77
00:04:56.060 --> 00:05:00.060
This allows you to render out videos from a camera

78
00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:06.759
or record game object properties during play or simulation.

79
00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:10.699
This is really useful for recording physics simulations

80
00:05:10.699 --> 00:05:13.399
or game objects that are animated via the timeline

81
00:05:13.399 --> 00:05:15.240
or animated via script.

82
00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:19.000
If you know you have something set and it doesn't need to change

83
00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:21.199
and you just need to get it over quickly

84
00:05:21.199 --> 00:05:24.910
and you don't want to do all of that same work,

85
00:05:24.910 --> 00:05:28.240
we can use the recorder.

86
00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:30.949
Let's look over here in Unity.

87
00:05:30.949 --> 00:05:34.449
This cube that's floating will now make a little more sense.

88
00:05:36.019 --> 00:05:43.019
We have our Rigidbody enabled, so when we press Play,

89
00:05:43.019 --> 00:05:47.500
look up here, we'll see our cube fall to the ground.

90
00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:52.040
What if we wanted to bring that cube in and have that same animation logic

91
00:05:53.639 --> 00:05:57.740
or have this same simulation play exactly the way that we want it to?

92
00:05:58.839 --> 00:06:03.339
We can do this over here in the Unity Recorder.

93
00:06:03.339 --> 00:06:05.199
Here we have our cube selected.

94
00:06:05.199 --> 00:06:09.639
We want to record its transform into an FBX.

95
00:06:09.639 --> 00:06:12.699
We don't need its hierarchy.

96
00:06:12.699 --> 00:06:17.339
We would like the geometry source.

97
00:06:17.339 --> 00:06:21.040
Let's drag in, drag this in again.

98
00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:24.290
Actually, let's leave this as not.

99
00:06:24.290 --> 00:06:27.910
Here we have the name of the animation file,

100
00:06:27.910 --> 00:06:32.879
and it will be saved into assets folder.

101
00:06:32.879 --> 00:06:41.170
Recordings, it's okay.

102
00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:48.569
Take number two, and I'll hit Start Recording.

103
00:06:48.569 --> 00:06:52.120
Stop recording, awesome.

104
00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:54.410
So now if we look here,

105
00:06:54.410 --> 00:07:01.720
we can see our cube animation waiting for us.

106
00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:12.790
Now let's get to importing our assets into Unreal.

107
00:07:12.790 --> 00:07:14.829
When importing static meshes for this,

108
00:07:14.829 --> 00:07:18.050
we're mostly just going to leave it to the default settings.

109
00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:21.889
There is an option to combine separate meshes into one,

110
00:07:21.889 --> 00:07:24.430
but for us, in this instance, leaving them separate

111
00:07:24.430 --> 00:07:27.389
makes it easier to make adjustments to them individually,

112
00:07:27.389 --> 00:07:31.089
which we'll do later on in the session.

113
00:07:31.089 --> 00:07:34.379
So we'll go Import.

114
00:07:34.379 --> 00:07:38.360
We will look for our exports.

115
00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:43.459
Session stage objects open.

116
00:07:43.459 --> 00:07:46.959
Source name for asset is good.

117
00:07:46.959 --> 00:07:52.439
We can apply different translation or rotation offsets.

118
00:07:52.839 --> 00:07:54.339
Static meshes import.

119
00:07:54.339 --> 00:07:58.800
We do not want them to be combined.

120
00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:02.660
Import collision, that all looks good, okay.

121
00:08:02.660 --> 00:08:12.389
Import, it looks like they all came in together

122
00:08:12.389 --> 00:08:15.589
to start with, but we can see that our separate materials

123
00:08:15.589 --> 00:08:18.889
did not come through, and they all just share one

124
00:08:19.730 --> 00:08:25.180
calibration mat of the platform.

125
00:08:25.180 --> 00:08:34.399
So let's bring in now our session demo dancer.

126
00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:43.419
In this case, we will again leave everything to blank,

127
00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:48.879
but for the animations, or everything to default,

128
00:08:48.879 --> 00:08:56.399
but in animations, I believe we can.

129
00:08:56.399 --> 00:08:59.480
This snap to closest frame boundary

130
00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:01.320
will help prevent some warnings we get

131
00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:05.299
during the import process.

132
00:09:05.299 --> 00:09:12.570
Import, and here we see all of our dancer assets.

133
00:09:12.570 --> 00:09:14.529
There's quite a few.

134
00:09:14.529 --> 00:09:19.320
We have our skeletal mesh here is waiting for us,

135
00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:23.879
but it appears she is facing the other direction.

136
00:09:23.879 --> 00:09:30.570
See if we drag her in.

137
00:09:30.570 --> 00:09:35.730
She's facing over to, how can we fix that?

138
00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:38.029
So when importing skeletal mesh assets,

139
00:09:38.870 --> 00:09:40.570
we also are gonna be able to leave this

140
00:09:40.570 --> 00:09:44.129
mostly to default for now, but one thing to note

141
00:09:44.129 --> 00:09:47.570
is that we want to have import animations checked

142
00:09:47.570 --> 00:09:51.090
and snap to closest frame boundary also checks

143
00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:56.559
to help prevent warnings on import.

144
00:09:56.559 --> 00:09:58.120
When we're all done, these should be

145
00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:01.419
all the different assets that we see.

146
00:10:01.419 --> 00:10:04.179
In our case, we have all of our cubes combined

147
00:10:04.179 --> 00:10:07.120
and stage assets combined together,

148
00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:12.690
so we can work on splitting those apart a little bit later.

149
00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:16.129
Once again, we can save everything by clicking the button

150
00:10:16.129 --> 00:10:18.490
in the bottom right corner of that.

151
00:10:20.980 --> 00:10:24.620
We noticed though that when we dragged our character in,

152
00:10:24.620 --> 00:10:26.960
they are now facing to the right.

153
00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:29.399
This is due to a difference in Unity

154
00:10:29.399 --> 00:10:33.100
and Unreal's coordinate axes,

155
00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:38.379
although this is changing in an upcoming version

156
00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:42.019
of Unreal again, but it should make this transition

157
00:10:42.019 --> 00:10:44.379
between the two programs a little bit easier.

158
00:10:45.879 --> 00:10:49.179
For now though, if we want to have our character

159
00:10:49.179 --> 00:10:52.850
come in facing the correct direction,

160
00:10:52.850 --> 00:10:57.850
what we need to do is go to our skeletal mesh here,

161
00:10:57.950 --> 00:11:01.259
report mesh with dialogue.

162
00:11:01.259 --> 00:11:06.379
Then here, let's see if we change our Z rotation by 90

163
00:11:06.879 --> 00:11:11.919
and import what that looks like,

164
00:11:14.889 --> 00:11:16.610
and now she's facing the right way.

165
00:11:16.610 --> 00:11:17.809
Amazing.

166
00:11:17.809 --> 00:11:20.929
And we want to go ahead and make sure we save her.

167
00:11:20.929 --> 00:11:22.250
There we go.

168
00:11:22.250 --> 00:11:24.970
So next, let's drag in our static meshes

169
00:11:24.970 --> 00:11:28.679
into our level.

170
00:11:28.679 --> 00:11:33.559
So here we can drag in our stage objects.

171
00:11:33.559 --> 00:11:37.610
I'm gonna put it here so that I can situate it

172
00:11:37.629 --> 00:11:42.870
around our platform here.

173
00:11:42.870 --> 00:11:45.330
So I think that'll look cool.

174
00:11:45.330 --> 00:11:47.019
Awesome.

175
00:11:47.019 --> 00:11:49.259
And then again, in this case,

176
00:11:49.259 --> 00:11:51.340
instead of re-importing the static mesh,

177
00:11:51.340 --> 00:11:54.980
I'm just going to rotate it 90 degrees

178
00:11:54.980 --> 00:11:57.740
using its transform component here.

179
00:11:57.740 --> 00:11:59.879
I think that will give us,

180
00:11:59.879 --> 00:12:02.899
or that'll get it lined up nice and right.

181
00:12:02.899 --> 00:12:21.639
And this will look really cool.

182
00:12:21.639 --> 00:12:23.450
All right, not too bad.

183
00:12:23.450 --> 00:12:26.049
If you want to check in the same way in Unity,

184
00:12:26.629 --> 00:12:29.590
you can go into orthographic views up here

185
00:12:29.590 --> 00:12:31.809
where the perspective camera is.

186
00:12:31.809 --> 00:12:33.269
This dropdown is in Unreal.

187
00:12:33.269 --> 00:12:35.149
This is where we can also go up

188
00:12:35.149 --> 00:12:39.350
to look at an orthographic view inside of the viewport.

189
00:12:40.309 --> 00:12:44.029
By default, everything is this wireframe view.

190
00:12:44.029 --> 00:12:45.870
If you'd like to switch it from wireframe additionally,

191
00:12:45.870 --> 00:12:50.870
here you can go and see it lit or unlit rendering.

192
00:12:50.909 --> 00:12:52.230
But I generally find,

193
00:12:52.230 --> 00:12:54.269
when I'm going to line things up perfectly up here,

194
00:12:54.269 --> 00:12:58.059
it's kind of nice to have it in the wireframe view.

195
00:12:58.059 --> 00:13:01.570
You can go back to our perspective.

196
00:13:01.570 --> 00:13:05.419
Fantastic.

197
00:13:05.419 --> 00:13:06.740
In the next video,

198
00:13:06.740 --> 00:13:08.620
we'll be taking a look at how to create

199
00:13:08.620 --> 00:13:10.179
different kinds of blueprints.
